<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vistula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wisła</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vistula is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, about 1,050 kilometres long, rising in the Silesian Beskids and flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> to the Baltic near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. Its basin has been the core of the Polish lands since the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and was earlier home to Germanic and Baltic peoples. Grain shipped down the river to Gdańsk was the basis of Polish trade from the 15th to the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vistula.jpg" alt="Vistula" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mariusz Guć · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolina-dolnej-wis%C5%82y.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vistula is 1,047 km long and the longest river draining into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It rises at Barania Góra in the Silesian Beskids, at 1,220 metres above sea level, and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, Sandomierz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, Płock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, Bydgoszcz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. Near the sea it forms a wide delta, the Żuławy Wiślane, whose channels include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a>. Its basin covers 193,960 km2, of which 168,868 km2 lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The river took its present shape within the last 14,000 years, after the Scandinavian ice sheet withdrew, and it gives its name to the last glaciation of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Vistulian or Weichselian.</p><p>The name first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> in 40 CE and as Vistla in Pliny in 77 CE; Ptolemy wrote Ouistoula. It is often derived from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning to ooze or flow slowly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the first millennium BCE large parts of the basin were occupied by the Iron Age Lusatian and Przeworsk cultures. Roman authors included the western part among the lands of Magna Germania, and Ptolemy in the 2nd century CE described the river as the border between Germania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a>. Slavs settled the estuary in the 7th and 8th centuries.</p><p>West Slavic Polish tribes formed small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> from the 8th century, and the ninth-century document of the Bavarian Geographer lists the Vistulans in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wislica">Wiślica</a> as their main centres. Polish legend ties the river to the beginnings of the state: according to Jan Długosz, Princess Wanda, daughter of King Krak, drowned herself in it rather than marry a German prince.</p><p>For centuries the Vistula was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s main trading arteries, carrying salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, grain and building stone between the 10th and 13th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, invited in 1226 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a>, controlled the lower river in the 14th century and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> castle in 1308. In the 15th century Gdańsk became a great centre of trade. Grain shipped on the river rose from 14,000 in 1491 to 310,000 in 1618, and Gdańsk, at the river's end, became the wealthiest and largest centre of crafts in the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The river is associated with Polish culture and national identity, flows through the two main cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and gives the phrase Land on the Vistula, which can mean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It is navigable from the Baltic to Bydgoszcz, though navigation upstream is limited. Severe floods over the centuries sometimes depopulated land in the delta, which had to be resettled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vistula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He invaded Pannonia during the Wilhelminer War of 882–884 with “troops from all the Slav lands”, seized a pagan prince on the <b>Vistula</b>, and in 892 withstood King Arnulf's invasion although Magyar horsemen rode with the Franks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Commonwealth’s economy rested on large agricultural surpluses, above all from the <b>Vistula</b> basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The province took in Pomerelia with the mouth of the <b>Vistula</b> and the city of Danzig, the Chełmno and Michałów Lands with Thorn, the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia with Frauenburg, Heilsberg and Allenstein, and the Marienburger Land with Marienburg and the city of Elbing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1230 the Landmeister Hermann Balk led the knights across the <b>Vistula</b>, founding Thorn in 1231, Kulm in 1232 and Elbing in 1237.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For the West Slavs between the <b>Vistula</b> and the Elbe, the chronicles of Thietmar of Merseburg, Adam of Bremen and Helmold, the lives of Otto of Bamberg, Saxo Grammaticus’ Gesta Danorum and the Knýtlinga saga describe temples and cults in detail.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Rhine Beaker groups met the Corded Ware culture, and from there they spread east as far as the <b>Vistula</b> in Poland; another expansion reached Csepel Island in Hungary by about 2500 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
